From f956523edce14840a651c8533311a5acdf53e3a4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?=EC=9C=A0=ED=98=84=EC=84=9D?= <evermongwan@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 05:44:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Portenta C33: let the UWB Shield and Wi-Fi share SPI1

The ESP32-C3 Wi-Fi link and the UWB Shield both sit on SPI1 (P100-P102).
ESPhost opened its own FSP instance of the same channel and lwIpWrapper
ran lwip_task() (and the ESP SPI traffic) from a 100 Hz timer interrupt,
so ESP transfers could land in the middle of a UWB transfer.

- ESPhost: talk to the ESP32 through the shared SPI1 ArduinoSPI object
  (ESPHOST_USE_ARDUINO_SPI, default on), restore the previous SPISettings
  after each transfer, add weak bus/handshake/control-wait hooks.
- SPI: add getSettings(), make beginTransaction() atomic, add
  spi_set_keep_open() so a user's end() cannot close a shared bus.
  Object layout and vtable unchanged (libUWBShieldApi.a is precompiled).
- lwIpWrapper: weak recursive-lock hooks around lwip_task(), the socket
  wrappers and sync requests; setExternalTaskMode() to drive lwip_task()
  from an RTOS task instead of the timer IRQ; poll-yield hook in DNS,
  DHCP, ping and TCP wait loops.
- Arduino_FreeRTOS: 48 KB heap on the C33 (UWB stack + coexistence task).

All hooks are no-ops by default; behaviour without a coexistence layer is
unchanged apart from ESPhost using SPI1.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01A3m3TFiwD564ej3SMfu7jv
